---
/** Statewide hub for the Florida funeral-cost survey: the four metros side by side, the state rules, and the method. */
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { links } from '../config/site';
import { longDate } from '../i18n/ui';
import { checkedMonth, checkedOn, metros, money, nfda, providersFor, rowsFor, statewide, stats } from '../lib/survey';

const rows = metros.map((m) => ({ m, dc: stats(rowsFor(m.slug, 'direct cremation').map((r) => r.price)), providers: providersFor(m.slug).length }));
const all = stats(rows.flatMap(({ m }) => rowsFor(m.slug, 'direct cremation').map((r) => r.price)));
const totalProviders = rows.reduce((s, r) => s + r.providers, 0);
const officialFees = metros.filter((m) => m.me.fee).map((m) => m.me.fee as number);

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the cheapest way to handle a funeral in Florida?',
    a: '<p>Direct cremation, with no viewing or service first, is usually the least expensive choice. The published prices we found in four Florida metros started at about $800, before the medical examiner’s fee and death certificates. A memorial service can still be held later, at home or at a place of worship.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I have to buy a casket for a cremation?',
    a: '<p>No. Under the FTC Funeral Rule, a funeral home that offers direct cremation must let you use a simple container, such as unfinished wood or heavy cardboard, or a container you provide.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Florida require embalming?',
    a: '<p>No, as long as the body is refrigerated. Florida requires refrigeration or embalming once 24 hours have passed after death.</p>',
  },
];
---
<Guide
  title="Florida Funeral & Cremation Costs by City"
  h1="What funerals and cremation cost in Florida"
  description={`Published funeral and cremation prices from ${totalProviders} providers in Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville and Miami, checked ${checkedMonth}, plus Florida's rules and fees.`}
  path="/florida-funeral-costs/"
  crumb="Florida funeral costs"
  eyebrow="Funeral costs"
  lede={`What funeral homes and cremation providers in four Florida metros publish online, checked ${checkedMonth}, plus the state rules and county fees that apply.`}
  published="2026-11-07"
  reviewed="2026-11-07"
  summary={[
    `Published direct cremation prices ran from ${money(all.low)} to ${money(all.high)} across the four metros we checked.`,
    `Every Florida cremation needs the medical examiner's approval. The official fees we found were ${money(Math.min(...officialFees))} to ${money(Math.max(...officialFees))}.`,
    'Florida law bars cremation until 48 hours after death.',
    `NFDA's ${nfda.year} median for our region: ${money(nfda.southAtlanticBurial)} for a funeral with a viewing and burial, ${money(nfda.southAtlanticCremation)} with cremation, before cemetery costs.`,
  ]}
>
  <h2>Four Florida metros side by side</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Published direct cremation prices and local fees, {checkedMonth}</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Metro</th>
          <th scope="col">Direct cremation</th>
          <th scope="col">Middle price</th>
          <th scope="col">Medical examiner fee</th>
          <th scope="col">Death certificate</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(({ m, dc }) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={`/funeral-costs-${m.slug}/`}>{m.city}</a></th>
            <td data-label="Direct cremation">{money(dc.low)} to {money(dc.high)} ({dc.n} providers)</td>
            <td data-label="Middle price">{dc.median ? money(dc.median) : 'Too few prices'}</td>
            <td data-label="Medical examiner fee">{m.me.fee ? money(m.me.fee) : '$50 or $75 (not official)'}</td>
            <td data-label="Death certificate">{money(m.deathCert.first)} first copy</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Each city page has the full local picture: funeral packages, county help for families who can't pay, and the nearest national cemeteries.</p>
  <ul>
    {metros.map((m) => <li><a href={`/funeral-costs-${m.slug}/`}>Funeral and cremation costs in {m.city}</a></li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Florida rules every family runs into</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Medical examiner approval.</strong> The district medical examiner must approve every cremation, and charges a fee for it (<a href={statewide.meApproval.source} rel="noopener">{statewide.meApproval.cite}</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>A 48-hour wait.</strong> No cremation until 48 hours after death (<a href={statewide.wait48.source} rel="noopener">{statewide.wait48.cite}</a>).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Who signs.</strong> A "legally authorized person" authorizes the cremation in writing. The person's own written wishes come first
      (or someone they named on a military form), then, in order, a surviving spouse, an adult child, a parent and an adult sibling
      (<a href={statewide.authorization.source} rel="noopener">{statewide.authorization.cite}</a>).
    </li>
    <li><strong>Refrigeration or embalming</strong> is required after 24 hours, so embalming isn't required if the body is refrigerated (<a href={statewide.refrigeration.source} rel="noopener">{statewide.refrigeration.cite}</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Prices by phone.</strong> Under the <a href={statewide.ftc} rel="noopener">FTC Funeral Rule</a>, funeral homes must give prices over the phone, and an itemized price list to anyone who asks in person.</li>
    <li><strong>Complaints.</strong> Florida's Department of Financial Services oversees funeral homes and cemeteries: {statewide.dfs.tollFree} (in Florida) or {statewide.dfs.phone}.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Help with the cost</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Veterans and their spouses</strong> can be buried in a national cemetery. The VA covers the grave, opening and closing, liner, headstone and perpetual care, but not funeral home charges, a casket or urn, or cremation. <a href={statewide.vaCovers} rel="noopener">VA burial benefits</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Social Security</strong> pays a one-time $255 to an eligible surviving spouse or child. <a href={links.ssaDeath} rel="noopener">Details at ssa.gov</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>County programs</strong> exist in some counties for families who can't pay, usually for a basic cremation. Each city page lists what we found.</li>
    <li><strong>Final expense insurance</strong> is a small whole life policy that pays your family in cash for these costs. <a href="/final-expense-insurance-florida/">How it works in Florida</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>About this survey</h2>
  <p>
    We gathered the prices {totalProviders} funeral homes and cremation providers publish on their websites and price lists, as shown in search
    results on {longDate(checkedOn, 'en')}. These aren't quotes, and this isn't a phone survey. Many price lists are undated, a few providers
    show different prices on different pages, and "starting at" prices often leave out the medical examiner's fee, death certificates and an
    urn. Ranges count each provider's listed price once per service. We show a middle price only where we found five or more. We have no
    business relationship with any provider, and a listing isn't a recommendation. The sources are on each city page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The NFDA medians come from the <a href={nfda.source} rel="noopener">National Funeral Directors Association's {nfda.year} price study</a>
    (national: {money(nfda.nationalBurial)} with burial, {money(nfda.nationalCremation)} with cremation). They leave out cemetery, marker and
    cash-advance charges.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions about funeral costs in Florida</h2>
  <Faq items={faqs} />
</Guide>
